Wizard's "Microsoft Entra sign-in configuration" step showed both district.local and
raytakosharkygmail.onmicrosoft.com as "Not Added" against the tenant's verified domains,
blocking Next until the "Continue without matching all UPN suffixes to verified domains"
override was checked.

district.local: expected, non-routable, and no real account carries this UPN suffix anymore
since the restamp exercise. Not a concern.

raytakosharkygmail.onmicrosoft.com: genuinely unexplained. Checked against the actual tenant
state (not assumed) via the Entra admin center, signed in as breakglass@raytakosharkygmail.onmicrosoft.com:
Identity > Domain names > Custom domain names showed raytakosharkygmail.onmicrosoft.com,
Status: Available (green check), spelled identically to the AD UPN suffix. No typo, no case
mismatch, genuinely verified on Microsoft's side.

RULED OUT:
- Stale wizard cache: fully exited and relaunched the whole Entra Connect application, signed
  in fresh as breakglass. Identical "Not Added" result both times.
- Missing connectivity to Graph/sign-in endpoints: from VM102, qm guest exec ->
  login.microsoftonline.com:443 True, graph.microsoft.com:443 True, graph.windows.net:443 True.
- A specific "provisioningapi.microsoftonline.com" endpoint gap: this was Claude's own
  hypothesis, named from memory rather than a verified source, and it was WRONG. Resolve-DnsName
  for that hostname returned nothing both via DC01's forwarder AND via 1.1.1.1 directly (a
  resolver with no relationship to this lab) - the hostname does not exist in public DNS at all.
  Retracted explicitly rather than left standing.

NOT YET EXPLAINED: why the wizard reports "Not Added" for a domain independently confirmed
Available in the tenant. No further evidence-backed hypothesis was available without more
guessing, so the decision (Raymond's) was to proceed past it via the wizard's own override
checkbox rather than keep diagnosing.

CONSEQUENCE ACCEPTED KNOWINGLY: the override's own warning states users will not be able to
sign in to Microsoft Entra ID with on-premises credentials if the UPN suffix does not match a
verified domain. Since every real user account in district.local now carries the
raytakosharkygmail.onmicrosoft.com suffix (per the restamp exercise), if the wizard's warning
is actually correct rather than a false negative, EVERY real user would fail hybrid sign-in.
This has NOT been tested yet.

OPEN QUESTION FOR THIS REPORT, HIGH PRIORITY: after sync completes, test whether a real
synced user account (e.g. jsmith) can actually sign in to Microsoft Entra ID / Microsoft 365
with their on-premises credential. This is the authoritative test of whether the wizard's
warning was a real problem or a false negative — nothing short of an actual sign-in attempt
settles it.
